<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<sdf version='1.9'>
  <model name='x500_gimbal_lidar_thermal'>
    <self_collide>false</self_collide>

    <include merge='true'><uri>model://x500</uri></include>

    <!-- Gimbal (unchanged: keeps joint names + command topics the bridge uses) -->
    <include merge='true'>
      <uri>model://gimbal</uri>
      <pose>0 0 0.26 0 0 3.14</pose>
    </include>
    <joint name="GimbalAttachJoint" type="fixed">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>cgo3_mount_link</child>
    </joint>

    <!-- Lidar rangefinder, RE-PARENTED onto the gimbal end-effector.
         Link name + sensor name MUST stay lidar_sensor_link / lidar for the bridge. -->
    <joint name="lidar_gimbal_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent>camera_link</parent>       <!-- was base_link in x500_lidar_down -->
      <child>lidar_sensor_link</child>
    </joint>
    <link name="lidar_sensor_link">
      <!-- Match the camera sensor's orientation so the ray (+X) points where the camera looks.
           Camera sensor pose rel. to camera_link is "-0.0412 0 -0.162 0 0 3.14"; this link
           sits 32.8 mm behind it in x, which is a mounting offset, not a boresight error. -->
      <pose relative_to="camera_link">-0.074 0 -0.162 0 0 3.14</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>0.001</mass>
        <inertia><ixx>0.00001</ixx><iyy>0.00001</iyy><izz>0.00001</izz>
                 <ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz><iyz>0</iyz></inertia>
      </inertial>
      <sensor name='lidar' type='gpu_lidar'>
        <gz_frame_id>lidar_sensor_link</gz_frame_id>
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <update_rate>50</update_rate>
        <ray>
          <scan>
            <horizontal><samples>1</samples><resolution>1</resolution>
                        <min_angle>0</min_angle><max_angle>0</max_angle></horizontal>
            <vertical>  <samples>1</samples><resolution>1</resolution>
                        <min_angle>0</min_angle><max_angle>0</max_angle></vertical>
          </scan>
          <range><min>0.1</min><max>1000.0</max><resolution>0.01</resolution></range>
        </ray>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <visualize>true</visualize>
      </sensor>
    </link>

    <!-- ===================== THERMAL CAMERA =====================
         Mounted on the gimbal end-effector, same as the lidar above, but pulled in as a
         NESTED INCLUDE rather than inlined as a new link.

         WHY NESTED: single source of truth. thermal_cam carries a lot of hard-won
         configuration - the L16/resolution/max_temp relationship, the quantisation
         ceiling, the deliberate absence of a <topic>, the 1 g stub mass. Inlining the
         sensor here would fork all of that, the way gimbal_invis had to fork
         model://gimbal and now carries a keep-in-sync warning. Including it means
         retuning the camera once updates every vehicle that carries it.

         HISTORY, because the comment used to say something else: the nested include was
         originally forced, because thermal_cam's link was also called camera_link and
         would have collided with the gimbal's under <include merge='true'>. That link has
         since been renamed to thermal_camera_link - DART flattens the vehicle into one
         Skeleton and names BodyNodes by unscoped leaf name, so SDF scoping alone still
         produced a duplicate-name auto-rename in the log. With the link renamed there is
         no longer any collision, so merge='true' WOULD now work and would drop the extra
         model/thermal level from the topic path. Left nested deliberately: the mount is
         verified boresighted to a third of a thermal pixel with these exact include
         semantics, and switching to merge changes how the pose is resolved, which would
         need re-verifying for no functional gain.

         Verified working: `gz model -m x500_gimbal_lidar_thermal` reports
         thermal_gimbal_joint with parent camera_link and child
         thermal::thermal_camera_link.

         POSE: identical to the gimbal camera sensor's own pose, so the thermal core is
         CO-BORESIGHTED with the RGB camera - zero parallax, which makes thermal/RGB pixel
         correspondence exact and is the main thing you want for fusion work in SITL. It
         is not physical: a real dual-payload gimbal has a side-by-side baseline. Add one
         by offsetting y here (45 mm is about one housing width, and at 20 m that is well
         under one thermal pixel of parallax, so it costs nothing but realism).

         The two bodies interpenetrate geometrically, which is deliberate and harmless:
         all of thermal_cam's geometry lies within 34 mm of its origin, inside BOTH the
         RGB camera's 0.05 m near clip and the thermal camera's own 0.1 m near clip, so
         neither camera renders the other's housing. Only an external viewport sees it.

         NOT CO-FRAMED, though: the gimbal RGB is 2.0 rad / 1280x720 and the thermal is
         0.8727 rad / 320x256, so the thermal image is a narrow centre crop of the RGB
         view. Realistic for a thermal core, but do not expect 1:1 pixel mapping without
         a homography.

         AIRFRAME IS VISIBLE TO THIS CAMERA AND CANNOT BE HIDDEN. This model includes
         model://gimbal, not model://gimbal_invis - but that would not help anyway:
         thermal sensors ignore visibility_mask/visibility_flags (measured against an RGB
         positive control), so the x500 frame and T-bar landing gear occlude thermal
         detections and the fork's usual trick does not apply. Fix it with gimbal
         pointing, not with visibility flags. -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://thermal_cam</uri>
      <name>thermal</name>
      <pose relative_to="camera_link">-0.0412 0 -0.162 0 0 3.14</pose>
    </include>
    <joint name="thermal_gimbal_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent>camera_link</parent>
      <child>thermal::thermal_camera_link</child>
    </joint>
  </model>
</sdf>
